<!--
    GH-9218: "Cannot properly adjust input varnodes" (mc68k link/unlk frame)
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/9218

    Source:  a small mc68k function with a standard link/unlk frame that reads
             two overlapping stack parameters:
               link.w  A6,#0
               movea.l (0xa,A6),A1     ; reads a 4-byte stack long  (-> stack:6 size 4)
               cmpi.w  #1,(0x8,A6)      ; reads a 2-byte stack word  (-> stack:4 size 2)
               ble.b   .. ; movea.l (0x4,A1),A0 ; move.b (1,A0),D0 ; cmp.b (A5),D0
               beq.b .. ; bcs.b .. ; unlk A6 ; rts
             (bytes verbatim below; 68000:BE:32:default)

    Default output (bug):  the decompile ABORTS with the low-level error
        Low-level ERROR: Cannot properly adjust input varnodes
      (funcdata_varnode.cc:508, Funcdata::adjustInputVarnodes).

    Root cause: ActionUnjustifiedParams (coreaction.cc) re-justifies the 2-byte
    word at stack:4 into the model's 4-byte int container [stack:4, stack:7].
    Its overlap-widening loop only absorbs input Varnodes that overlap and extend
    BELOW the container start; the 4-byte long at stack:6 (spanning [6,9])
    overlaps the container and extends ABOVE its end (byte 9 > 7) and is never
    absorbed.  adjustInputVarnodes then hits
        vn->getOffset()+(vn->getSize()-1) > endaddr.getOffset()  (9 > 7)
    and throws, aborting the whole function.

    Stage model: S6 storage reconciliation, sub-stage `stack-frame-layout`.
    P0 assertion `option inputvarnodeadjust on|off` (default off = upstream
    byte-identical, including the abort).  When on, the overlap-widening loop is
    made symmetric: an input Varnode overlapping the container and extending
    above its end widens the container to enclose it, so adjustInputVarnodes pulls
    all intersecting inputs out as SUBPIECEs of one new (6-byte) input and the
    function decompiles.

    NOTE on harness shape: the default-pipeline failure is an ABORT.  The upstream
    test harness (FunctionTestCollection::runTests) returns early when the console
    enters an error state, so an aborting decompile yields NO bulk output and the
    <stringmatch> rules are never evaluated (a default/bug pass therefore cannot
    be asserted in a passing file; it fails the whole file).  This testcase instead
    asserts the FIX directly under `option inputvarnodeadjust on`: the function
    recovers to a single merged input parameter (`param_1`) accessed via SUBPIECE
    (`param_1._2_4_`), and the abort token is proven ABSENT.  Removing the option
    line reproduces the bug (file fails with "Cannot properly adjust input
    varnodes"); see the repro command in the issue record.
-->
